Supply the null import descriptor in import libraries (issue #135)

Microsoft link.exe does not terminate the import directory table on its
own. It relies on import libraries to supply the terminator: each import
descriptor references __NULL_IMPORT_DESCRIPTOR, whose archive member
contributes 20 zero bytes in .idata$3, sorted after every descriptor in
.idata$2. Binutils import libraries lack this member, so when link.exe
is given only Binutils import libraries it quietly produces an image with
an unterminated import directory, running into the .idata$4 lookup table
that follows. GNU ld and lld are unaffected because they emit their own
terminator.

Do the same as MSVC: make dlltool and ld --out-implib add an archive
member defining __NULL_IMPORT_DESCRIPTOR, and reference it from the head
member. A link pulls in exactly one copy no matter how many import
libraries are involved, and an MSVC import library's definition serves
just as well. Delay-import libraries are unchanged, as is ld's direct
linking against DLLs, which doesn't go through an import library.

